board meetings generally the first and third Mondays with a couple of Mondays January and February is being kicked midmon to Tuesdays because of uh equality day Martin Luther King Day and also President's Day and Memorial Day sometimes falls on a Monday No I think it's kicked our meeting once before maybe I don't know Um our expectation of the board is we want to make sure that you all are running the meeting fairly and not to lose control of of meetings You all haven't had anything majorly contentious in a while where you've had a a audience that's been out of control Even if you do support an item you know personally and stuff you know re be sure to maintain your composure You don't want to be agreeing with the audience going "Yeah that's awesome Joey I love you Joey that's the right

way to go You don't want to want to do that You just want to be the uninfluenced decision maker Uh respect respect staff and each other We haven't had any problems since 2018 So no I'm just kidding We haven't had any problems with that but just remember to respect staff because a lot of times people may have differing viewpoints on different things Um we ask that you're consistent in your um decisions consistent in time limits and when you put time limits on folks for public comment And the other thing that a lot of folks struggle with especially when you have public comment that comes up to different meetings is you need to make sure that you're basing your decisions on like the applicable review criteria Um

this is a one-sizefits-all all slide with our board of adjustment where they they call it findings of fact Um but here we just need to make sure that they meet the u review criteria Um just because something is generally opposed by the public you know that's that's very common that you're going to see that argument a lot Um a lot of times as a planning commission you're simply making a recommendation to the governing body They're the ones who have that paycheck although it's small um they're the ones who are getting paid as the electors to make those political decisions that are more based on the um public comment So it's it's always best to uh

try to stick to the to the review criteria and public comment is one that should be incorporated Um but just a general opposition because of the usual uh things we hear It's too much traffic It's going to decrease property values is going to block my view of this that intersections done It's going to flood my property You know those are ones we're going to hear on almost every project and those should be taken Um not saying they should be dismissed but they should not be the only factor in your decisions Um so we come in the meetings we usually understand what's going to happen but sometimes we leave and we're like "Okay that happened." But whatever your recommendation is um

our role as staff is to carry forward your recommendation to the governing body for approval So say you modify text amendment Tuesday morning or Wednesday morning we have to turn it around to the governing body We get with our city attorney's office the other departments and divisions that may be affected and we have to reddraft that ordinance And we will clearly state that in a cover memo this is how it changed at planning commission and these are the reasons why or this is the reason they recommended you deny it was for these solid reasons So whenever you do say something's getting denied tell us why Um because just trying to understand your discussion isn't going to be enough And

